set-bg: img/paper.jpg
new-page: true

A chapter in history closes.

- @republic_endures
- @coup_defeated
- @long_civil_war
- @republic_falls

@republic_endures
title: The Republic Endures
subtitle: Ending 1
view-if: not republic_victory and not long_war and not total_defeat
new-page: true

July 1936 comes and goes without a rising. Whatever plotting occupied the garrisons and the Carlist strongholds of Navarre never coheres into an open move against Madrid - the conspirators wait for an opportunity that keeps slipping past them, and the moment for a clean coup is gone.

[? if in_popular_front : The Popular Front government carries on, its coalition as fractious as ever - the PSOE's Prietista and Caballerista wings still arguing over whether to have joined the cabinet at all, the PCE pressing from the left, the CEDA and the monarchists fuming from the right. It is not a comfortable peace, but it is a peace. ?][? if in_radical_ceda : The Radical-CEDA government continues to govern, the PSOE still in opposition, land reform still stalled, the *bienio negro* still bitter - but no shots have been fired over it. ?][? if in_republican_socialist : The Republican-Socialist coalition's reform program - land, army, Church, Catalonia - continues, contested at every turn but never overturned by force. ?]

Spain does not become a byword for civil war. It remains what it has been since April 1931: an argument, conducted mostly in the Cortes and occasionally in the streets, about what kind of country it wants to be. That argument is still not settled - the generals have not gone away, the CNT has not stopped waiting for its revolution, the CEDA has not stopped dreaming of a corporatist state - but for now, it stays an argument rather than a war.

- @go_back

@coup_defeated
title: We Have Won the War
subtitle: Ending 2
view-if: republic_victory
new-page: true

The rising fails. Whatever the rebel generals expected - a swift, near-bloodless seizure of power like Primo de Rivera's in 1923 - they do not get it. In Madrid and Barcelona, armed workers and loyal troops hold the streets within days; the promised general rising of the garrisons stalls in isolated pockets, mostly in the south and in Africa, cut off from each other and from any hope of relieving one another.

Franco's Army of Africa, unable to cross the Straits against a Republican navy still largely loyal, is bottled up in Morocco. Mola's northern rising, denied the quick capture of the Basque coast, withers under pressure from Asturian miners and Basque nationalist forces alike who have no love for a military dictatorship, whatever their differences with Madrid on other questions.

Spain has been changed by the violence of that July - militias that fought in the streets do not simply disband, and the balance between the government, the parties, and the armed organizations that saved it will be argued over for years. But the Republic survives, on its own terms, in a country it did not have to share with a military regime.

- @go_back

@long_civil_war
title: A Country Divided
subtitle: Ending 3
view-if: long_war
new-page: true

Neither side is strong enough to finish the other quickly. By the autumn of 1936, Spain has split along a rough line running from the Basque coast down through Madrid's approaches to the Mediterranean: a Nationalist zone under the rebel generals, built on the Army of Africa, the Requetés, and the Falange; a Republican zone held together by the government, the UGT and CNT militias, and whatever remains loyal of the Army and the Guardia Civil.

The war that follows is not the swift affair either side hoped for. Foreign intervention deepens the divide rather than resolving it - sympathizers abroad send what aid they can, rarely enough to be decisive, always enough to keep both sides fighting. Whole provinces change hands more than once. The cost, in lives and in the fabric of the country, mounts far past what anyone expected in the confusion of that first July week.

How it ends - a negotiated settlement, an outright victory for one side, a peace imposed by exhaustion rather than by force of arms - is a story for another day. What is certain is that Spain in 1936 chose, or was forced into, the one outcome nearly everyone in the Republic's five years of argument had hoped to avoid.

- @go_back

@republic_falls
title: The Republic Has Fallen
subtitle: Ending 4
view-if: total_defeat
new-page: true

The rising succeeds too quickly and too completely for the Republic's defenders to organize. The Army of Africa crosses the Straits with the Navy's resistance broken or bypassed; garrison after garrison declares for the rebels before the government can arm enough of the UGT and CNT militias to make a fight of it in the cities that matter. Madrid falls, or is encircled and starved into surrender, within weeks rather than years.

What replaces the Second Republic is not a restoration of the old monarchy but something new: a military dictatorship built on the wartime authority of the generals who won, leaning on the Church, the landowners, and whatever remains of the CEDA and the monarchist right willing to serve it. The reforms of 1931-1936 - land redistribution, secular education, regional autonomy, army reform - are undone in short order. The PSOE, the UGT, the PCE, and the CNT-FAI are banned; their leaders are imprisoned, executed, or flee across the Pyrenees.

It is, at least, a swift defeat rather than a long war - Spain is spared the years of attrition that a more evenly matched rising might have produced. Small comfort to the Republic's defenders, for whom the argument that began in April 1931 has just been settled, decisively and for a long time to come, by force.

- @go_back

